# TODO - Turnkey C-to-Malbolge toolchain

## Objective

Deliver the final supported workflow: bootstrap every public project dependency
except Jig, accept an explicitly named C source file, and emit one verified
`.malbolge` artifact through a single documented command on supported Windows
and
Linux hosts. CPU compilation remains universally available; NVIDIA CUDA is an
optional accelerator on Windows and Linux and never changes semantic acceptance.

## Acceptance

- One repository command provisions or verifies pinned public dependencies,
  including Python, Rust, LLVM/Clang, C analysis tools, and optional manifest-
  selected CUDA user-space packages; Jig is not downloaded, installed, or
  required by the end-user bootstrap.
- Privileged operating-system prerequisites such as NVIDIA kernel drivers are
  detected and diagnosed with exact remediation guidance rather than installed
  silently or replaced by an unpinned system dependency.
- After bootstrap, one stable command such as
  `malbolge compile input.c -o input.malbolge` validates the guest-C profile,
  compiles the source, verifies the result, and atomically writes the requested
  `.malbolge` file without requiring the user to invoke intermediate stages.
- The CPU/reference path works without a GPU. A supported NVIDIA GPU may
  accelerate search or optimization on Windows or Linux, including headless
  Linux servers, while missing CUDA falls back explicitly to CPU execution.
- Re-running bootstrap and compilation is deterministic, offline-capable after
  the exact archives are cached, and produces provenance sufficient to reproduce
  the toolchain and artifact identity.
- End-to-end fixtures cover a minimal C program, byte I/O, an unsupported-C
  diagnostic, CPU-only compilation, Linux NVIDIA selection/fallback, and output
  path collision or partial-write failure.
- Completion requires all dependencies above and represents the user-facing
  project completion gate: a clean checkout can become a working C-to-Malbolge
  compiler without undocumented manual setup.

## Evidence

- Required durable artifacts: cross-platform bootstrap manifests, one public
  compile command, atomic output handling, end-to-end C/`.malbolge` fixtures,
  CPU-only evidence, and retained Linux NVIDIA pipeline evidence.
- Jig remains repository-governance tooling for maintainers and is explicitly
  outside the redistributable/end-user dependency contract until a separate
  publication decision exists.
- ROCm is not a completion dependency; its reserved adapter contract must remain
  unavailable rather than pretending to have an implementation or device
  evidence.
